findings.program:
class Counter:
    hits: int
    fn drop():
        println("closed")          # the clean-up

struct Link:
    target: shares mut Counter      # no `fn drop()` on `Link`

fn main():
    c = shares mut Counter(hits=0)
    frame = Arena.with_capacity(4096)
    l = ref mut frame.alloc(owns Link(target=shares mut c))   # <- is `Link` "a type that needs drop"? If "needs drop" means "has a `fn drop()`", this compiles: the owner count of the `Counter` is never subtracted, and `closed` never prints. The same for a `weakref` field (a weak count never subtracted, the block never freed) and for an `Array` field (its buffer never freed).

@derive(Zeroable)
struct Cmd:
    id: int

@realtime                                   # @noalloc @nolock @noblock @nopanic(explicit)
fn tick(ref mut fixed: FixedArena) -> int:
    c = owns Cmd(id=1) using fixed          # <- the effect table: `owns` before a value is "constructing an allocating type": `Alloc`, so E4001? [ARN-4]: a FixedArena "carries no `Alloc` on allocation". And `fixed.allocate` returns null when full ([ALC-2]): the compiler's own call then panics, as [ALC-4] says of containers ("out of memory") or as [ARN-4] says of the arena ("panics on exhaustion")? Is that "allocation failure", which `Panic(Explicit)` lists, or [PHIL-11]'s "exhaustion outside `Alloc`", which [EFF-16] says the model does not cover? Two compilers answer differently and accept different programs.
    return c.id

@no_alias_checks
fn halves(ref mut xs: MutSpan[float]):
    (lo, hi) = xs.split_at(xs.len() // 2)   # two halves the structural form proves apart ([DSJ-4])
    assert_disjoint(lo, hi)                 # <- by [DSJ-5] no compare is emitted and no `RuntimeCheck(Aliasing)` site is recorded, so no E4030; by [EFF-15] the verdict is computed "as if every one of them were applied" over exactly four named proofs, none of which is this one, so the compare counts and the line is E4030. Two conforming compilers disagree on whether `halves` compiles.
